# PreToolUse safety rules: block dangerous Bash commands
# Format: id@@regex@@message (delimiter: @@)
#   Optional 4th field: id@@regex@@message@@severity
#   Optional 5th field: id@@regex@@message@@severity@@explanation
#   Severity: critical (all profiles), standard (default), strict (strict only)
#   If omitted, defaults to "standard".
#   Explanation: shown when EXOSUIT_EXPLAIN_MODE=verbose (WHY + safer alternative)
# Each line's regex is checked against the command string via grep -E.
# Matched commands are blocked (exit 2) with the given message.
# Lines starting with # are comments. Empty lines are skipped.
#
# Git patterns use (-C\s+\S+\s+)? to catch flag workarounds like `git -C /path push --force`

# --- Git: destructive operations ---
git-push-force@@git\s+(-C\s+\S+\s+)?push\s+.*--force(\s|$)@@git push --force is not allowed. Use --force-with-lease if necessary.@@critical@@WHY: Force-push rewrites the remote branch history. If anyone has pulled your branch, their local copy will break — they'll get conflicts with no way to reconcile. INSTEAD: Use git push --force-with-lease which checks that nobody else pushed changes first, or use git revert to undo a commit safely.
git-push-f@@git\s+(-C\s+\S+\s+)?push\s+(.*\s)?-[a-zA-Z]*f[a-zA-Z]*(\s|$)@@git push -f is not allowed. Use --force-with-lease if necessary.@@critical@@WHY: The -f flag is shorthand for --force. It rewrites remote history and can destroy other people's work. INSTEAD: Use git push --force-with-lease (checks for upstream changes) or git revert (creates a new undo commit).
git-reset-hard@@git\s+(-C\s+\S+\s+)?reset\s+--hard@@git reset --hard is destructive. Use git stash or git reset --soft instead.@@critical@@WHY: reset --hard permanently discards ALL uncommitted changes — both staged and unstaged — with no recovery. Unlike most git operations, this data is gone forever. INSTEAD: Use git stash (saves changes for later, reversible) or git reset --soft HEAD~1 (undoes the commit but keeps all changes staged).
git-clean-f@@git\s+(-C\s+\S+\s+)?clean\s+(.*\s)?-[a-zA-Z]*f[a-zA-Z]*(\s|$)@@git clean -f permanently deletes untracked files. Review with git clean -n first.@@critical@@WHY: clean -f permanently deletes all untracked files (files not yet added to git). There is no undo. INSTEAD: Run git clean -n first to preview what would be deleted, then use git clean -fi for interactive confirmation.
git-branch-force-delete@@git\s+(-C\s+\S+\s+)?branch\s+.*-D@@git branch -D force-deletes a branch. Use git branch -d (safe delete) instead.@@critical@@WHY: -D force-deletes a branch even if it has unmerged commits — those commits become unreachable and will be garbage collected. INSTEAD: Use git branch -d which refuses to delete branches with unmerged work, protecting you from losing changes.
git-checkout-dot@@git\s+(-C\s+\S+\s+)?checkout\s+\.\s*$@@git checkout . discards all changes. Use git stash or commit first.@@critical@@WHY: checkout . silently discards all uncommitted modifications across every file. There is no confirmation and no undo. INSTEAD: Use git stash (reversible — get changes back with git stash pop) or commit your work first.

# --- Git: hook bypass ---
git-no-verify@@--no-verify@@--no-verify skips safety hooks. Fix the issue the hook caught instead.@@critical@@WHY: Hooks exist to catch real problems (secrets in code, failing tests, style violations). Skipping them doesn't fix the problem — it hides it. The issue will surface later, often in production. INSTEAD: Read the hook's error message, fix the underlying issue, then commit normally.

# --- Git: direct push to default branch ---
git-push-main@@git\s+(-C\s+\S+\s+)?push\s+\S*\s*main(\s|$)@@Direct push to main is not allowed. Create a pull request instead.@@critical@@WHY: Pushing directly to main bypasses code review and CI checks. If the code has bugs, they go straight to the main branch with no safety net. INSTEAD: Push to a feature branch and create a pull request — this lets CI run and others review before merging.
git-push-master@@git\s+(-C\s+\S+\s+)?push\s+\S*\s*master(\s|$)@@Direct push to master is not allowed. Create a pull request instead.@@critical@@WHY: Pushing directly to master bypasses code review and CI checks. INSTEAD: Push to a feature branch and create a pull request.

# --- Filesystem: destructive operations ---
rm-rf-git@@rm\s+-rf\s+\.git@@rm -rf .git destroys the repository. This is never allowed.@@critical@@WHY: Deleting .git removes all version history, branches, stashes, and configuration. The entire project history is permanently lost. INSTEAD: If you need to start fresh, clone the repo again.
rm-rf-dangerous@@rm\s+-rf\s+(/|\.\.|~)@@rm -rf on root, parent, or home directory is not allowed.@@critical@@WHY: Deleting /, .., or ~ destroys critical system files, the parent directory, or your entire home directory. This can make the system unbootable. INSTEAD: Specify the exact directory you want to delete by name.

# --- Package publishing (requires manual execution) ---
package-publish@@(npm\s+publish|cargo\s+publish|twine\s+upload|gem\s+push|pod\s+trunk\s+push)@@Package publishing requires manual execution outside Claude Code.@@critical@@WHY: Publishing packages is irreversible and affects all users of the package. A wrong publish can break downstream projects globally. INSTEAD: Run the publish command manually in your terminal after reviewing the package contents.

# --- Database: destructive operations ---
db-destructive@@(DROP\s+(TABLE|DATABASE)|TRUNCATE\s+TABLE)@@Destructive database operations. Run manually with review.@@critical@@WHY: DROP and TRUNCATE permanently delete data with no undo (unless you have a backup). In production, this means data loss for real users. INSTEAD: Run these commands manually with a review step, ideally on a staging database first.

# --- Process: mass killing ---
mass-kill@@(kill\s+-9\s+-1|killall\s|pkill\s+-9)@@Mass process killing is not allowed.@@critical@@WHY: Mass kill commands terminate multiple processes indiscriminately, potentially including critical system services, databases, or other development tools. INSTEAD: Use kill with a specific PID, or use pkill with a precise process name pattern (without -9 first — let processes clean up).
